# Cache-Control for Cloudflare Workers static assets.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/headers/
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# Workers Assets serves everything in here with `public, max-age=0,
# must-revalidate` by default. Measured on production before this file:
# /hero-poster-plate.jpg and every /images/*.jpg came back with exactly
# that, which Lighthouse reports as a cache lifetime of ZERO.
#
# It costs more than the source images. The optimized `_next/image`
# responses were uncached too, and NOT for a reason anyone would guess
# from the Next config: `images.minimumCacheTTL` DOES NOTHING on this
# adapter. The OpenNext Cloudflare build compiles it in as an esbuild
# define (cli/build/open-next/compile-images.js) and its own image
# handler never reads the global — grep __IMAGES_MINIMUM_CACHE_TTL_SEC__
# in cli/templates/images.js and there is one declaration in the .d.ts
# and no use. What that handler actually does is:
#
#   immutable = parseResult.static
#             || upstreamCacheControl.includes("immutable")
#
# and it sets Cache-Control ONLY when `immutable` came out true. Our
# photos are referenced as string paths in src/data/images.ts, not as
# static imports, so `static` is false and the whole decision falls to
# the header the source asset was served with — this file. Setting
# `immutable` here is therefore what caches BOTH the original and every
# optimized derivative of it; there is no separate knob for the latter.
#
# ⚠️ THE CONTRACT `immutable` MAKES, AND WHY WE CAN KEEP IT
#
# `immutable` promises the bytes at a URL will never change. The project
# already works this way and wrote the rule down before this file
# existed: see the dining-room note in src/data/images.ts, where a
# replacement photograph was given a NEW filename specifically because
# "writing new bytes under an unchanged URL means every cache keyed on
# that URL still holds the old picture". A photograph that is a
# different picture gets a different filename. That convention is what
# this header is standing on.
#
# So: to change any image below, ADD A FILE, do not overwrite one. An
# overwrite is now a year-long stale cache for returning visitors with
# no way to tell from the page.

# ⚠️ THIS ONE IS NOT BELT-AND-BRACES, IT IS THE ONLY BRACES.
#
# Next's build output is content-hashed by construction — every file
# under _next/static carries a hash of its own contents in the name — so
# on a host that knows what Next is, these ship immutable automatically.
# Workers Assets does not know what Next is. Measured on the built worker
# before this rule:
#
#   /_next/static/chunks/<hashed>.js -> public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
#
# which means every returning visitor was revalidating every chunk and
# every stylesheet on every navigation, for files that cannot change
# without changing their URL. This was checked rather than assumed
# precisely because "it should be immutable by default" is true almost
# everywhere else.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/hero-poster-plate.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# NOTE: the hero footage is NOT here. It is an R2 object on
# media.newmandarincantonii.com, and R2 serves whatever cacheControl was
# written at upload time rather than anything in this file — see
# docs/DEPLOY_RUNBOOK.md for the upload, which sets the same year-long
# immutable value against a content-hashed key.

/brand/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Brand marks and icons, all content-stable under the same rename rule.
/*.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# NOT immutable, and deliberately so: this is a document about the site
# that is meant to be re-read, and agents fetch it precisely to find out
# what changed. A day is long enough to be worth caching and short
# enough that a corrected fact reaches them.
/llms.txt
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
